          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The D’Auria-Fré-Regge formalism (due to
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#NeemanRegge78"} Ne’eman &amp; Regge 1978
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DAuriaFréRegge79"} D’Auria, Fré &amp; Regge 1979
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DAuriaFréRegge80a"} 80a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DAuriaFréRegge80b"} 80b
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#CastellaniDAuriaFre"} Castellani, D’Auria &amp; Fré 1991
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            on “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superspace"}> superspace
            ” (with some hindsight: in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"} higher
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartan+geometry"} super Cartan geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensions"} dimensions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D4+supergravity"} D=4 supergravity
            also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+supergravity"} heterotic supergravity
            in dimension 10 as well as notably
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
          %p
            This proceeds in generalization of how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            is equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            for the inclusion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            inside the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+group"} Poincare group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            configuration of the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            is equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
            for this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            inclusion.
          %p
            Accordingly, low dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            without
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+supersymmetry"} extended supersymmetry
            is equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-Cartan+geometry"} super-Cartan geometry
            of the inclusion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin group
            into the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} super Poincaré group
          %p
            What D’Auria-Fré implicitly observe (not in this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            language though, that was developed in
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#SSS"} Sati, Schreiber &amp; Stasheff 09
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FiorenzaSchreiberStasheff10"} Fiorenza, Schreiber &amp; Stasheff 10
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FSS"} 13
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+supersymmetry"} extended supersymmetry
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+super+Lie+algebra"} M-theory super Lie algebra
            symmetry, the description of the fields is in the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            version of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"} higher Cartan geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincare+Lie+algebra"} super Poincare Lie algebra
            is replaced by one of its exceptional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-infinity+algebra"} super Lie n-algebra
            extensions (those that also control the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+scan"} brane scan
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+6-algebra"} supergravity Lie 6-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-Cartan+geometry"} super-Cartan geometry
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"} higher Cartan geometry
          %p
            This
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"} higher
            = succeed "-description" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartan+geometry"} super Cartan geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            is what D’Auria-Fré called the
            %em geometric approach to supergravity
            or
            %em geometric supergravity
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DAuria20"} D’Auria 20
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
                    context
