            see also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+theory+introduction"} ∞-Chern-Weil theory introduction
          %h3#history History
          %p
            Around 1981 D’Auria and Fré noticed, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DAuriaFre"} GeSuGra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theories
            have a strikingly powerful reformulation in terms of super
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree differential graded-commutative algebra
          %p
            They defined various such super dg-algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(\mathfrak{g})
            and showed (paraphrasing somewhat) that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the field content,
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identity"} Bianchi identities
                are all neatly encoded in terms of dg-algebra homomorphism
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo •
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ←
                        %mi W
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi ϕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet(X) \leftarrow W(\mathfrak{g}) : \phi
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                may be constructed as images under
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                of certain elements in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(\mathfrak{g})
                subject to natural conditions.
          %p
            Their algorithm was considerably more powerful than earlier more pedestrian methods for construction such action functionals. The textbook
            %a{:href => "#CastellaniDAuriaFre"} CastellaniDAuriaFre
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            from the perspective of this formalism gives a comprehensive description of this approach.
          %p
            We observe here that the D’Auria-Fre-formalism is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theory"} ∞-Chern-Simons theory
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra-valued+forms"} ∞-Lie algebra-valued forms
            with values in super
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+6-algebra"} supergravity Lie 6-algebra
          %p
            The pivotal concept that allows to pass between this interpretation and the original formulation is the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
            with its various incarnations:
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (Incarnations of
                = succeed "-Lie" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %p
              A (super)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  is an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
                  (super)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+groupoid"} ∞-Lie groupoid
              %li
                %p
                  that may be modeled as a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial
                  (super)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal space
              %li
                %p
                  whose
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebras+on+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} function algebra
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosimplicial+algebra"} cosimplicial
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+algebra"}> super
                  ) algebra
              %li
                %p
                  that under the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Dold-Kan+correspondence"} monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence
                  maps to a (super)
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree differential graded-commutative algebra
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
                  of the (super)
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
          %p
            Notably the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree dga
            upon which D’Auria-Fré base their description is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            that is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra+cohomology"} higher central extension
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲𝔤𝔯𝔞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 10
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 10
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  0 \to b^2 \mathfrak{u}(1)
                  \\to \mathfrak{sugra}(10,1)
                  \\to
                  \\mathfrak{siso}(10,1)
                  \\to
                  0
          %p
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincare+Lie+algebra"} super Poincare Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 10
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{siso}(10,1)
            in the way the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+Lie+2-algebra"} String Lie 2-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{string}(n)
            is a higher central extension of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+Lie+algebra"} special orthogonal Lie algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔰𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{so}(n)
          %p
            A super
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} connection on an ∞-bundle
            with values in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲𝔤𝔯𝔞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 10
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{sugra}(10,1)
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifold"} supermanifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is locally given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid+valued+differential+forms"} ∞-Lie algebroid valued differential forms
            consisting of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 11
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{11}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                – the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein"} vielbein
            %li
              %p
                a
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔰𝔬
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 10
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{so}(10,1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                – the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+connection"} spin connection
            %li
              %p
                a spin-representation valued 1-form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
                – the spinor
            %li
              %p
                a 3-form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                \.
          %p
            These are identified with the fields of 11-dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graviton"} graviton
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi e
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (e, \omega)
            %li
              %p
                the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitino"} gravitino
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            %li
              %p
                the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                \.
          %p By realizing this data as components of a Lie 3-algebra valued connection (more or less explicitly), the D’Auria-Fré-formalism achieves some conceptual simplication of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the construction of supersymmetric
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                = succeed "s;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %li
              %p the determination of the corresponding classical equations of motion.
          %h3#higher_gauge_theory_reinterpretation Higher gauge theory reinterpretation
          %p
            Originally D’Auria and Fré referred to commutative
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree dga
            = succeed "." do
              %em Cartan integrable systems
            = succeed "," do
              %em free differential algebra
            %em FDA
            was used instead and became popular. Nowadays much of the literature that studies commutative
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree dga
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            refers to them as “FDA”s. One speaks of the
            %em FDA approach to supergravity
            \.
          %p
            But strictly speaking “free differential algebra” is a misnomer: genuinely free differential algebras are pretty boring objects. Crucially it is
            %em only
            the underlying graded commutative algebra which is required to be free as a graded commutative algebra in that it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge^\bullet \mathfrak{g}^*
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^*
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree dga
            \.
          %p
            In fact, when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is concentrated in non-positive degree (so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge^\bullet \mathfrak{g}^*
            is concentrated in non-negative degree) the differential on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge^\bullet \mathfrak{g}^{*}
            encodes all the structure of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is concentrated in negative degree the differential encodes the structure of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree dga
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            is the key to our
            %em general abstract reformulation
            of the D’Auria-Fré-formalism.
          %p
            Already D’Auria and Fré themselves, and afterwards other authors, have tried to better understand the intrinsic conceptual meaning of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            formalism that happened to be so useful in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
          %p
            The idea arose and then became popular in the “FDA”-literature that the D’Auria-Fré-formalism should be about a concept called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soft+group+manifold"}> soft group manifold
                s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            formulation the fields in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            arrange themselves into systems of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maurer-Cartan+form"} Maurer-Cartan form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            –
            %em except
            that where the ordinary Maurer-Cartan form has vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            (=
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
          %p
            However, even when the field strengths
            %em do
            vanish, the remaining collection of differential forms does not constrain the base manifold to be a group. Rather, if the field strengths vanish we have a natural interpretation of the remaining differential form data as being flat
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid+valued+differential+forms"} ∞-Lie algebroid valued differential forms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A : T X \to \mathfrak{g}
          %p
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+Lie+algebroid"} tangent Lie algebroid
            of the base
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            encoded by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree dga
            in question. In fact, applying the functor from
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X)
                  \\leftarrow
                  CE(\mathfrak{g})
                  \:
                  A
          %p
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+dg-algebra"} deRham dg-algebra
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (which we denote by the same letter,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g})
            is semifree, this is a map of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X)
                  \\leftarrow
                  \\mathfrak{g}^*
                  \:
                  A
          %p
            together with a constraint that the morphism respects the differentials on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g})
            and on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet(X)
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega \in \Omega^\bullet(X,\mathfrak{g})
          %p
            and the aforementioned constraint is precisely the Maurer-Cartan-like equation that is known from left-invariant 1-forms on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            there is a canonical morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(G)
                  \\leftarrow
                  CE(\mathfrak{g})
          %p
            whose image is precisely the left-invariant 1-forms on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and whose respect for the differentials is precisely the ordinary Maurer-Cartan equation.
          %p To see the role of group manifolds for more general morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X)
                  \\leftarrow
                  CE(\mathfrak{g})
                  \:
                  A
          %p
            one has to apply
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
            morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X \to \mathfrak{g}
            to a morphism of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+groupoid"} ∞-Lie groupoid
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Pi(X) \to \mathbf{B}G
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} path ∞-groupoid
            and where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Lie+groupoid"} Lie in-group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            that integrates the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+n-algebra"} Lie n-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid+valued+differential+forms"} ∞-Lie algebroid valued differential forms
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+theory"} ∞-Chern-Weil theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections+on+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} connections on ∞-bundles
            is about
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the generalization of such flat form data to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid+valued+differential+forms"} ∞-Lie algebroid valued differential forms
                with
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            %li
              %p
                the generalization from globally defined differential form data – which are connections on
                %em trivial
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
          %p
            The D’Auria-Fré-formalism – after this re-interpretation – is about the first of these points. So as an immediate gain of our reformulation of D’Auria-Fré-formalism in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections+on+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} connections on ∞-bundles
            we obtain, using the second of these points, a natural proposal for a formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            field configurations that are possibly globally topologically nontrivial. Physicists speak of
            = succeed "." do
              %strong instanton solutions
          %p
            In fact, the
            = succeed "-reformulation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+theory"} ∞-Lie theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            as a higher
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
          %p
            It realizes supergravity as an example for a
            %em nonabelian
            higher gauge theory in that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            field configuration is not realizable as a cocycle in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            as in ordinary abelian higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            (see there) but as a nonabelian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} connection on an ∞-bundle
          %h2#kinematics Kinematics
          %h3#the_supergravity_lie_algebras
            The supergravity Lie
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            We have a sequence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra+cohomology"} ∞-Lie algebra extensions
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+6-algebra"} supergravity Lie 6-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincare+Lie+algebra"} super Poincare Lie algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔰𝔲𝔤𝔯𝔞
                    %mn 6
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔰𝔲𝔤𝔯𝔞
                    %mn 3
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 10
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathfrak{sugra}_6 \to \mathfrak{sugra}_3 \to \mathfrak{siso}(10,1)
                  \\,.
          %p …
          %h3#super_lorentzian_spacetime_manifolds Super Lorentzian spacetime manifolds
          %p
            The base space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            on which a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            field is a super Lie
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} connection on an ∞-bundle
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifold"} supermanifold
          %p
            In particular, for constructing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            we want
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to locally look like
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+space"} super Minkowski space
          %h3#field_configuration_and_field_strength Field configuration and field strength
          %p
            A local field configuration on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifold"} supermanifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            is a morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi inn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲𝔤𝔯𝔞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T X \stackrel{(A, F_A)}{\to}
                  inn(\mathfrak{sugra}(\mathfrak{g}))
          %p
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+Lie+algebroid"} tangent Lie algebroid
            to the inner-derivation Lie 4-algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi inn
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔰𝔲𝔤𝔯𝔞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 10
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} inn(\mathfrak{sugra}(10,1))
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+algebra"} Weil algebra
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔰𝔲𝔤𝔯𝔞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{sugra}
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+algebra"} Weil algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲𝔤𝔯𝔞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 10
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(\mathfrak{sugra}(10,1))
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+dg-algebra"} deRham dg-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet(X)
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲𝔤𝔯𝔞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 10
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X)
                  \\leftarrow
                  W(\mathfrak{sugra}(10,1))
                  \:
                  (A,F_A)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid+valued+differential+form"} ∞-Lie algebroid valued differential form
            data with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} ∞-Lie algebroid valued curvature
            that is explicitly given by:
          %ul
            %li
              %p connection forms / field configuration
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi E
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mrow
                              %mn 10
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \in \Omega^1(X,\mathbb{R}^{10,1})
                    – the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein"} vielbein
                    (part of the
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %em
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graviton"} graviton
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi 𝔰𝔬
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 10
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega \in \Omega^1(X, \mathfrak{so}(10,1))
                    – the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+connection"} spin connection
                    (part of the
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %em
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graviton"} graviton
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi \in \Omega^ 1(X,S)
                    – the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
                    (the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitino"} gravitino
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \in \Omega^3(X)
                    – a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+3-form"} differential 3-form
                    (the
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
                forms /
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi T
                          %mo =
                          %mi d
                          %mi E
                          %mo +
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mi E
                          %mo +
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mover
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mrow
                              %mn 10
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T = d E + \Omega \cdot E + \Gamma(\bar \Psi \wedge \Psi) \in \Omega^2(X,\mathbb{R}^{10,1})
                    \- the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+of+a+metric+connection"} torsion
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                          %mo =
                          %mi d
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo +
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi 𝔰𝔬
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 10
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = d \Omega + [\Omega \wedge \Omega] \in \Omega^2(X, \mathfrak{so}(10,1))
                    \- the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+curvature"} Riemann curvature
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ρ
                          %mo =
                          %mi d
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo +
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho = d \Psi + (\Omega \wedge \Psi) \in \Omega^2(X, S)
                    – the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
                      of the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi G
                          %mo =
                          %mi d
                          %mi C
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi μ
                            %mn 4
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ψ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi E
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mn 4
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = d C + \mu_4(\psi, E) \in \Omega^4(X)
                    – the
                    %strong 4-form field strength
          %h3#GaugeTransformations Gauge transformations
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
            of a field configuration
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi inn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi : T X \to inn(\mathfrak{g})
          %p is a diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ×
                          %msup
                            %mi Δ
                            %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi vert
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi A
                                %mi vert
                        %mtd
                          %mi CE
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi gauge
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi transformation
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ×
                          %msup
                            %mi Δ
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi field
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %msub
                                %mi F
                                %mi A
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mi inv
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔤
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi gauge
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi invariant
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi observable
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X \times \Delta^{1})_{vert}
                  \&amp;\stackrel{A_{vert}}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                  CE(\mathfrak{a})
                  \&amp;&amp;&amp;
                  gauge\;transformation
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X \times \Delta^{1})
                  \&amp;\stackrel{A}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                  W(\mathfrak{a})
                  \&amp;&amp;&amp;
                  field
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X)
                  \&amp;\stackrel{\langle F_A\rangle}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                  inv(\mathfrak{g})
                  \&amp;&amp;&amp;
                  gauge\;invariant\;observable
                  }
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Given a 1-morphism in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\mathfrak{g})(X)
              = succeed "-valued" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ←
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Omega^\bullet(U \times \Delta^1)
                    \\leftarrow
                    W(\mathfrak{g})
                    \:
                    A
            %p consider the unique decomposition
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi U
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi vert
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi d
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A = A_U + ( A_{vert} \coloneqq \lambda \wedge d t)  \; \;
                    \\,,
            %p
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_U
              the horizontal differential form component and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t : \Delta^1 = [0,1] \to \mathbb{R}
              the canonical coordinate.
            %p
              We call
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
              the
              %strong gauge parameter
              \. This is a function on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^1
              with values in 0-forms on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              an ordinary
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-algebra"} Lie 2-algebra
          %p We describe now how this enccodes a gauge transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A_0(s=1) \stackrel{\lambda}{\to} A_U(s = 1)
                  \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The condition that all
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature+characteristic+form"} curvature characteristic form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              in that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              completes to a diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mo •
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi U
                            %mo ×
                            %msup
                              %mi Δ
                              %mi k
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ←
                              %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mi W
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi 𝔞
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mo •
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ←
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                                %msub
                                  %mi F
                                  %mi A
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mtd
                            %mi inv
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi 𝔤
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Omega^\bullet(U \times \Delta^k)
                    \&amp;\stackrel{A}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                    W(\mathfrak{a})
                    \\\
                    \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow
                    \\\
                    \\Omega^\bullet(U)
                    \&amp;\stackrel{\langle F_A\rangle}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                    inv(\mathfrak{g})
                    }
            %p is solved by requiring all components
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi A
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi r
                          %mi a
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                    %msubsup
                      %mi F
                      %mi A
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Omega^\bullet(U \times \Delta^1)
                    \\stackrel{A}{\leftarrow}
                    W(\mathfrak{g})
                    \\stackrel{r^a}{\leftarrow}
                    \\wedge^1 \mathfrak{g}^*
                    F_A^a
            %p
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
              forms to vanish when evaluated on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+field"} vector field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_s
              along
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_s
            %p
              By the nature of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+algebra"} Weil algebra
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mi d
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi s
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi U
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi U
                    %mi λ
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi s
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{d}{d s} A_U
                    \=
                    d_U \lambda + [\lambda \wedge A] + [\lambda \wedge A \wedge A] + \cdots
                    +
                    (F_A)(\partial_s, \cdots)
                    \\,,
            %p
              so that this condition is a system of ordinary
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mi d
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi s
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi U
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi U
                    %mi λ
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{d}{d s} A_U
                    \=
                    d_U \lambda + [\lambda \wedge A] + [\lambda \wedge A \wedge A] + \cdots
                    \\,,
            %p
              where the sum is over all higher brackets of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Define the
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
                of the gauge parameter
              to be
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi d
                    %mi λ
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\nabla \lambda \coloneqq d \lambda + [A \wedge \lambda] + [A \wedge A \wedge \lambda] + \cdots
                    \\,.
          %p In this notation we have
          %ul
            %li
              %p the general identity
              #eq:ShiftedGaugeTrafo.maruku-equation
                %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mi d
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mi s
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi U
                      %mo =
                      %mo ∇
                      %mi λ
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi A
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\frac{d}{d s} A_U = \nabla \lambda + (F_A)_s
            %li
              %p
                the
                %strong horizontality
                constraint or
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ehresmann+connection"} second Ehresmann condition
              #eq:GaugeTrafo.maruku-equation
                %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mi d
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mi s
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi U
                      %mo =
                      %mo ∇
                      %mi λ
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\frac{d}{d s} A_U = \nabla \lambda
                      
                      \\,.
          %p
            This is known as the equation for
            %strong
              infinitesimal
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
            of an
            = succeed "-Lie" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              By
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
              we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
